    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A monk named Le Zun, or Yuezun, is said to have had a vision of a thousand Buddhas at the site in 366 CE, and caves continued to be made there until the 14th century.</li><li>In 1900 the Taoist abbot Wang Yuanlu found a walled-up cave holding an estimated 50,000 documents in Chinese, Tibetan, Sogdian, Khotanese and other languages, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diamond-sutra">Diamond Sutra</a> of 868.</li><li>Expeditions from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> removed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and paintings from 1907 to 1914, and the collections are now divided among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and other places.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The small seated Buddhas that cover whole walls in many caves, and give the site its common name, were drawn with stencils so that identical figures could be repeated.</p></aside>
    </section>

